### Why is modulo hashing a problem for scaling?

With plain modulo hashing, changing the node count reshuffles almost every key.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class ModuloHashDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int oldNodes = 4;
        int newNodes = 5;
        int moved = 0;
        for (int key = 0; key < 10000; key++) {
            int oldShard = Math.abs(key) % oldNodes;
            int newShard = Math.abs(key) % newNodes;
            if (oldShard != newShard) moved++;
        }
        System.out.println("Keys moved: " + moved + " / 10000");
        // Output: Keys moved: ~8000 / 10000 (80% reshuffled!)
    }
}
```

For a live banking system, moving 80% of data during a scale-out is catastrophic.

### How does consistent hashing work?

Both nodes and keys are hashed onto the same circular ring. A key is owned by the first node found clockwise from the key's position.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

import java.util.*;

public class ConsistentHashRing {

    private final TreeMap<Long, String> ring = new TreeMap<>();

    public void addNode(String node) {
        long hash = hash(node);
        ring.put(hash, node);
    }

    public void removeNode(String node) {
        long hash = hash(node);
        ring.remove(hash);
    }

    public String getNodeForKey(String key) {
        long hash = hash(key);
        Map.Entry<Long, String> entry = ring.ceilingEntry(hash);
        if (entry == null) {
            entry = ring.firstEntry(); // wrap around the ring
        }
        return entry.getValue();
    }

    private long hash(String value) {
        // Use a good hash function (MD5, MurmurHash, etc.)
        return value.hashCode() & 0xFFFFFFFFL;
    }
}
```

### How does adding a node minimize data movement?

Adding a node inserts one point on the ring and steals only the arc between it and the previous node.

```text
Before adding Node D:
       Node A (0°)
         │
         ▼
  ───────●──────────────────────────●─── Node B (120°)
         │                          │
         │    keys in this arc      │
         │    belong to Node B      │
         │                          │
  ───────●──────────────────────────┘
       Node C (240°)

After adding Node D at 60°:
       Node A (0°)   Node D (60°)
         │               │
         ▼               ▼
  ───────●───────────●──────────────●─── Node B (120°)
                     │
         Only keys between A and D
         move from B to D
         = K/N of total keys
```

### What is a virtual node?

A virtual node is one of many ring positions assigned to the same physical node.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

import java.util.*;

public class VirtualNodeHashRing {

    private final TreeMap<Long, String> ring = new TreeMap<>();
    private final int virtualNodes;

    public VirtualNodeHashRing(int virtualNodes) {
        this.virtualNodes = virtualNodes;
    }

    public void addNode(String physicalNode) {
        for (int i = 0; i < virtualNodes; i++) {
            long hash = hash(physicalNode + "#" + i);
            ring.put(hash, physicalNode);
        }
    }

    public void removeNode(String physicalNode) {
        for (int i = 0; i < virtualNodes; i++) {
            long hash = hash(physicalNode + "#" + i);
            ring.remove(hash);
        }
    }

    public String getNodeForKey(String key) {
        long hash = hash(key);
        Map.Entry<Long, String> entry = ring.ceilingEntry(hash);
        if (entry == null) entry = ring.firstEntry();
        return entry.getValue();
    }

    private long hash(String value) {
        return value.hashCode() & 0xFFFFFFFFL;
    }
}
```

With 150 virtual nodes per physical node, ownership is split into many small arcs that average out to an even share.

### Why do virtual nodes improve balance?

With few points per node, one node can accidentally own a much larger slice of the ring.

```text
Without virtual nodes (3 physical nodes):
  Node A: 45% of keys  ◄── hotspot!
  Node B: 30% of keys
  Node C: 25% of keys

With 150 virtual nodes per physical node:
  Node A: 33.2% of keys
  Node B: 33.5% of keys
  Node C: 33.3% of keys  ◄── balanced!
```

Heterogeneous nodes can be weighted by giving bigger machines more virtual nodes.

### How does consistent hashing enable replication?

Walk clockwise from the key's position to the next N distinct physical nodes for replicas.

```text
Key K hashed to position 45°
  → Primary: Node A (next clockwise physical node)
  → Replica 1: Node B (next distinct physical node)
  → Replica 2: Node C (next distinct physical node)
```

This is how Cassandra places replicas automatically.

### How does National Bank of India use consistent hashing?

Pankaj routes sharded accounts by account id on a consistent-hash ring.

```java
package com.bank.sharding;

public class AccountRouter {

    private final VirtualNodeHashRing ring;

    public AccountRouter() {
        ring = new VirtualNodeHashRing(150);
        ring.addNode("shard-mumbai-1");
        ring.addNode("shard-delhi-1");
        ring.addNode("shard-bangalore-1");
    }

    public String getShardForAccount(long accountId) {
        return ring.getNodeForKey(String.valueOf(accountId));
    }

    // Adding a new shard relocates only ~K/N accounts
    public void addShard(String shardName) {
        ring.addNode(shardName);
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Consistent hashing underpins Dynamo-style stores, Cassandra, and many distributed caches.
- Pick enough virtual nodes to keep load variance low (100-200 per physical node is common).
- Use a good hash function (MurmurHash3, xxHash) to avoid clustering.
- Replication is free by walking to the next N distinct physical nodes.
- When removing a node, the departing node's keys redistribute to its clockwise neighbor.
